From e6a46dc742cefd1a1f7a5c59e5e5e61a01e3f39c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 22:22:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] feat(risk): four-signal scoring for financial actions

Scores amount, geography, new-buyer and velocity into a low/medium/high band
whose only output is how many humans must approve. Risk cannot allow and
cannot refuse - a scorer that could block by itself is one scoring bug away
from refusing every transaction in the country.

Aggregation is dominant-signal-plus-corroboration rather than a weighted
average. An average lets one severe signal be diluted by three quiet ones: a
maxed-out geography flag averaged with three zeroes scores 0.25 and reads as
low, which is precisely backwards. The saturating term means extra signals
always raise the score but can never reach 1.0 alone, so weak evidence cannot
accumulate into certainty.

Signal thresholds are shared with the policy rules, so risk and policy cannot
drift into disagreeing about what fast or large means.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M6LL3Gr7HTQxMcJnbbKLzC
---
