From 1891167cc6643bf5f2e8bed4cf6bb2f06b1c4be1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 22:22:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] feat(authorization): the money gate - quorum, single use,
 scope binding

AuthorizationService runs Policy then Risk then records the verdict. consume()
is the gate every money path calls, and it refuses unless the record exists in
this merchant, is approved, is unexpired, is unspent, and the action presented
now fingerprints identically to the one approved.

The fingerprint is what makes the rest worth having. Without it an attacker
gets a small refund approved and presents that approval while charging lakhs -
a signed blank cheque.

Four properties, each with an adversarial test behind it: no self-approval, no
approver counted twice, single use, scope binding. Enforced in the service
rather than the router, so background jobs in later phases meet the same bar.

Denials are recorded, not discarded. A refusal that left no trace is invisible
to whoever investigates why a merchant's payment failed.

RBAC splits request, read and approve into three permissions so separation of
duties is expressible: support reads everything and approves nothing, and a
service account may ask but never bless its own request.

